{"reason": "The initial plan's core direction is to replace the static embedding + XGBoost pipeline with a fine-tuned transformer (DistilRoBERTa) to overcome the representational bottleneck. Historical experiments show that the previous attempt failed due to AdamW import error, but the transformer-based approach (Experiment 2) succeeded with proper imports and provided a working template. Additionally, the current XGBoost validation log loss of 0.6048 is far from the 0.3-0.5 range achieved by transformer models on this competition, confirming the need for the upgrade. To refine the plan: (1) model_design must be modified to correctly use DistilRoBERTa with proper AdamW import and adjusted hyperparameters (e.g., max_length=256 from the successful experiment); (2) data_processing must be simplified to remove handcrafted features and static embeddings, outputting raw text only (as planned), which also eliminates data leakage; (3) training_evaluation must incorporate gradient accumulation, mixed precision, and 5-fold cross-validation (as planned) but with early stopping and proper submission generation from the successful experiment. These three modules directly address the root cause and are informed by historical learnings.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Remove all handcrafted feature engineering, SentenceTransformer embeddings, scaling, and .npy saving. Instead, load train/test CSVs, apply label encoding on the author column, and output the raw text column along with labels and ids. Tokenization will be performed on-the-fly during training using the HuggingFace DistilRoBERTa tokenizer with max_length=256 (matching the successful historical experiment). This change eliminates data leakage (features were computed on combined train+test) and allows the transformer to learn directly from raw text. The module will save the processed DataFrames to pickle for the next stages.", "model_design": "Replace XGBoost with a DistilRoBERTa sequence classification model from HuggingFace. Use AutoModelForSequenceClassification with 3 output labels. Import AdamW from torch.optim (not transformers) to avoid the import error seen in historical experiment 1. Set dropout to 0.1, and use the same max_length=256 for tokenization. The optimizer will be AdamW with learning rate 2e-5, weight decay 0.01, and a linear warmup of 10% of steps with cosine schedule. This design ensures end-to-end fine-tuning on raw text, learning author-specific stylistic features directly.", "training_evaluation": "Implement 5-fold StratifiedKFold cross-validation. For each fold, train DistilRoBERTa using gradient accumulation (2 steps) and mixed precision (fp16) via PyTorch AMP or HuggingFace Accelerator (as in successful experiment). Use early stopping on validation log loss with patience of 3 epochs. Train for a maximum of 5 epochs per fold. At the end of each fold, generate test predictions and average across folds. Clip predictions to [1e-15, 1-1e-15], normalize rows to sum to 1, and save submission.csv with columns id, EAP, HPL, MWS. Print the final validation score as the last line."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan's core direction is to replace the static embedding + XGBoost pipeline with a fine-tuned transformer (DistilRoBERTa) to overcome the representational bottleneck. Historical experiments show that the previous attempt failed due to AdamW import error, but the transformer-based approach (Experiment 2) succeeded with proper imports and provided a working template. Additionally, the current XGBoost validation log loss of 0.6048 is far from the 0.3-0.5 range achieved by transformer models on this competition, confirming the need for the upgrade. To refine the plan: (1) model_design must be modified to correctly use DistilRoBERTa with proper AdamW import and adjusted hyperparameters (e.g., max_length=256 from the successful experiment); (2) data_processing must be simplified to remove handcrafted features and static embeddings, outputting raw text only (as planned), which also eliminates data leakage; (3) training_evaluation must incorporate gradient accumulation, mixed precision, and 5-fold cross-validation (as planned) but with early stopping and proper submission generation from the successful experiment. These three modules directly address the root cause and are informed by historical learnings.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Remove all handcrafted feature engineering, SentenceTransformer embeddings, scaling, and .npy saving. Instead, load train/test CSVs, apply label encoding on the author column, and output the raw text column along with labels and ids. Tokenization will be performed on-the-fly during training using the HuggingFace DistilRoBERTa tokenizer with max_length=256 (matching the successful historical experiment). This change eliminates data leakage (features were computed on combined train+test) and allows the transformer to learn directly from raw text. The module will save the processed DataFrames to pickle for the next stages.",
    "model_design": "Replace XGBoost with a DistilRoBERTa sequence classification model from HuggingFace. Use AutoModelForSequenceClassification with 3 output labels. Import AdamW from torch.optim (not transformers) to avoid the import error seen in historical experiment 1. Set dropout to 0.1, and use the same max_length=256 for tokenization. The optimizer will be AdamW with learning rate 2e-5, weight decay 0.01, and a linear warmup of 10% of steps with cosine schedule. This design ensures end-to-end fine-tuning on raw text, learning author-specific stylistic features directly.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement 5-fold StratifiedKFold cross-validation. For each fold, train DistilRoBERTa using gradient accumulation (2 steps) and mixed precision (fp16) via PyTorch AMP or HuggingFace Accelerator (as in successful experiment). Use early stopping on validation log loss with patience of 3 epochs. Train for a maximum of 5 epochs per fold. At the end of each fold, generate test predictions and average across folds. Clip predictions to [1e-15, 1-1e-15], normalize rows to sum to 1, and save submission.csv with columns id, EAP, HPL, MWS. Print the final validation score as the last line."
  }
}"}
